bbapprove doctor — diagnose the installation and the configuration.

Usage: bbapprove doctor [--offline] [--config FILE] [--disallow a,b]
       bbapprove doctor --help

Read-only: prints a report, changes nothing, never prints a secret, and needs no token,
forge access or config file. It also works when the configuration is broken: that is what it reports.

Options:
  --offline       skip the update check (nothing leaves this machine)
  --config FILE   diagnose this config file (BBAPPROVE_CONFIG does the same; the flag wins)
  --disallow a,b  add disallowed dependencies, as for a normal run
  -h, --help      show this help

Sections: one block each, in this order
  version      bbapprove version, platform, git checkout or release archive, the running binary,
               and whether the bbapprove on your PATH is that binary
  update       asks gh (or git in a checkout) for a newer release; skipped with --offline
  config       every candidate config file (--config, BBAPPROVE_CONFIG, repo root,
               $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bbapprove/config.toml) and which one wins, its mode, whether it is
               valid TOML and still holds the template's example values; then every effective
               setting with its source: [flag], [env], [file] or [default]
  adapter      the forge type, the adapter path, and that it exists and is executable
  token        whether BBAPPROVE_TOKEN is set (never its value, only its length)
  ignore list  the per-user ignore file, whether it is readable (an unreadable list is a problem:
               bbapprove then refuses to run), and its entries
  disallow     the disallow lists from the config file, BBAPPROVE_DISALLOW and the disallow file
  environment  the BBAPPROVE_* variables that are set, plus NO_COLOR, FORCE_COLOR,
               XDG_CONFIG_HOME, EDITOR and VISUAL
  tools        git and gh on PATH, and whether gh is logged in

Markers: ok / ✓ fine, warn / ! worth a look, FAIL / ✗ a problem.
The symbols replace the words on a colour terminal.

Exit: 0 all good (warnings allowed), 1 a problem was found, 2 bad usage.

Colour only on a terminal; NO_COLOR turns it off, FORCE_COLOR=1 forces it on.
